  "scriptId": "sim-14-pattern-products-defined-operations",
  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Patterns, Products, and Defined Operations",
    "description": "Spot cancelling chains, telescoping sums, and mixed-number shortcuts, and evaluate made-up operations defined in the question.",
    "teachingApproach": "Teach the four pattern families through reveal-first examples: diagonal cancellation in product chains, split-and-vanish telescoping sums, the near-a-round-number mixed-number rewrite, literal application of invented operations (including solving for an unknown inside one), and fixed step-recipe questions. The capstone fuses an invented operation with the difference-of-squares identity. Fresh question and parallel retry after each pattern.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 26,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 4, Simplification. Approved scope: Teach pattern recognition in simplification: product chains such as (1 − 1/3)(1 − 1/4)...(1 − 1/100) where each numerator cancels the next denominator leaving first denominator over last, telescoping sums such as 1/30 + 1/42 + ... + 1/110 using 1/(n(n+1)) = 1/n − 1/(n+1) so middle terms vanish, and mixed-number multiplication shortcuts such as 99 49/50 × 10 = (100 − 1/50) × 10. Cover operations invented by the question, such as x * y = 3x + 2y, evaluating expressions and solving equations like 2 ∧ a = a ∧ 3 by substituting the definition on both sides, plus multi-step instruction questions (Step I, Step II, Step III) where the learner applies a fixed recipe to a given number. Capstone: an invented operation a * b = a^2 − b^2 evaluated with the difference-of-squares identity, linking back to earlier learning. Learners should apply definitions literally and look for cancellation before multiplying anything out."
  },
  "startNodeId": "start",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Let's finish the chapter with its most playful questions.\n\nLong cancelling chains, sums that vanish in the middle, and operations the examiner invented this morning — all of them melt once you see the pattern.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Let's end the chapter with pattern-spotting — the laziest, most beautiful shortcuts.\n\n(1 − 1/3)(1 − 1/4)(1 − 1/5)...(1 − 1/100)\n\nNinety-eight brackets. Nobody multiplies them all.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1_step"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1_step": {
      "id": "teach_1_step",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Write each bracket as a fraction:\n\n2/3 × 3/4 × 4/5 × ... × 99/100\n\nWatch the diagonal: every top cancels the next bottom.\n\n3 cancels 3, 4 cancels 4, ... 99 cancels 99.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1_step2"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1_step2": {
      "id": "teach_1_step2",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Survivors: the very first bottom and the very last top.\n\n= 2/100 = 1/50\n\nFirst denominator over last numerator. That's the whole trick.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
